  "chunks": [
    {
      "chunk_type": "isnad",
      "arabic_text": "مُحَمَّدُ بْنُ يَعْقُوبَ عَنْ مُحَمَّدِ بْنِ يَحْيَى عَنْ أَحْمَدَ بْنِ مُحَمَّدٍ عَنْ عُثْمَانَ بْنِ عِيسَى عَنْ سَمَاعَةَ قَالَ",
      "translations": {
        "en": "Muhammad ibn Ya'qub from Muhammad ibn Yahya from Ahmad ibn Muhammad from 'Uthman ibn 'Isa from Sama'ah said:"
      }
    },
    {
      "chunk_type": "body",
      "arabic_text": "سَأَلْتُهُ عَنِ الْقَلْسِ وَ هُوَ الْجُشْأَةُ يَرْتَفِعُ الطَّعَامُ مِنْ جَوْفِ الرَّجُلِ مِنْ غَيْرِ أَنْ يَكُونَ تَقَيَّأَ وَ هُوَ قَائِمٌ فِي الصَّلَاةِ قَالَ «لَا يَنْقُضُ ذَلِكَ وُضُوءَهُ وَلَا يَقْطَعُ صَلَاتَهُ وَلَا يُفَطِّرُ صِيَامَهُ».",
      "translations": {
        "en": "I asked him about the qals (burping), which is the rising of food from a man's stomach without him vomiting, while he is standing in prayer. He said: 'That does not break his wudu, nor does it break his prayer, nor does it break his fast.'"
      }
    }
  ],
  "translations": {
    "en": {
      "summary": "This hadith provides a legal ruling regarding the act of burping (qals) during prayer and fasting. It clarifies that the rising of food from the stomach without full vomiting does not invalidate ablution (wudu), does not break the prayer (salat), and does not break the fast (sawm).",
      "seo_question": "Does burping break wudu, prayer, or fasting in Shia jurisprudence?",
      "key_terms": {
        "الْقَلْسِ": "Burping, specifically the rising of food from the stomach",
        "الْجُشْأَةُ": "The act of burping or belching",
        "وُضُوءَهُ": "His ablution (ritual purity)",
        "صلاة partial purification) that is not broken by this act"
